  user_input {
    user_response: "So predominantly, I don\'t care too much for trading, even though I wouldn\'t mind the function being available, as long as it\'s kind of spelled out and is dynamic, so it doesn\'t provide outdated information. I also think you should find a body of resources and sources to help you ground your information and your data and make sure it\'s up to date as of the current date and time because things change, not even just by the date but by the time in this game. So, players, new players come out, new promos, new team of the weeks. Different days means different competitions are available. One of the main things I want you to assist with is squad building, which means finding, we need to find the best way for you to keep up to date with my squad, the players that I have, and which players go, which players come in, the evolutions that are available, and how they relate to the players that I have. And the evolutions are available and you recommend, but maybe they don\'t fit some of the players that I have, so you, as a result, provide a certain amount of suggestions. And those suggestions should be based on the fact that where the players in my team that I already have or in my club already are based and their nationalities. Also just players that would be really good to chain. What else? Yes. Another thing is to keep a good eye on the amount of coins and FIFA points I have and how to use those coins and FIFA points, keep up to date with SBCs, how long is left with them, how much they cost, and then what\'s in my club. So we need to find the best ways to do that and see what is possible with regards to sinking and allowing you to keep up to date with my club basically, because previously I\'ve been relying on taking pictures and videos and uploading them, which is kind of long because the game is so dynamic and you\'re constantly having to do that and it can be an arduous experience. Consider I will be using things like Gemini Spark and Google Anti-Gravity to assist, and of course Google AI Pro and Google AI Studio. And so it\'s just about working out where and when and what and how and why for each of those things, how to use them together, and of course any other tools that you can find on Windows laptops or within the Google AI Pro subscription or open source tools, GitHub tools. I\'d like you to do a good search of GitHub with regards to EA Sports FC 27 Ultimate Team Console, just like repos and information or whatever, and see what\'s out there that we could add. I would also like you to do a little comparison to show what Futbin has and what the app that we have the plan to make has, and so point out the similarities and the differences. And then maybe do a little critique or an evaluation of Futbin and do the same for the app that we\'ve made and kind of show which one is the winner with regards to a certain sort of criteria or parameter. Then investigate what other apps are out there that support Ultimate Team EA FC 27 console and make sure, and the most important thing is to not use auto-regressive predictions or like heavily base answers on parameter weighting because of the nature of football is that there can be lots of data and high amounts of volume, sorry, high volumes of data for specific players because they played at a club for a long, long time, so there\'ll be lots more data for that player on the internet or wherever you get the data from. But as soon as they move, there\'ll be barely any data. And so if you use predictive and sort of parameter weighting, you will most likely miss that the player is no longer at the club or that a player has left the club. So that means not only do I want you to use Futbin as a dynamic and updated good database and platform for information about the game, but also use sites like FutMob, spelled F O T M O B, which is a very comprehensive free football app and website that has very up-to-date information about teams, squads, transfers, and who\'s in the current squad for a current season. You must consider that the latter half of the year will concern the start of the season and the beginning half of the year is the end of the season. So meaning if it\'s May 26, that means it\'s the end of the 25-26 season. If it\'s December 29, that means it\'s the first half of the 29-30 season. So yeah, if it\'s the end of the year, it means it\'s the start and therefore that number comes first in the season when you say the season. So if it\'s the end of the year, that means you say 29 and it\'s going to end in 2030. Whereas if it\'s the start of the year again, like if it\'s February, that means the season will end in the beginning half of the year. And so whatever year it is, for example, 2034, if it\'s 2034, that means it\'s the 2033-2034 season or 33-34 season. So that\'s just a rule of thumb to help you understand when a player might have moved and to make sure you\'ve got the correct season when you\'re looking. So you can kind of work it out right now. It\'s October and which means it\'s the end of the year. Therefore, we\'re towards the beginning of the season as the season starts in August and ends in May. So it\'s the beginning of the season meaning and it\'s the later part of the year meaning it\'s the 26-27 season. And just looking at data from a player from a certain year, like for example about Rodri, there\'ll be lots of information about him in 2026 because he\'s a massive player and lots of information about him from Manchester City because he played for them for a very long time for seven years. And so if you use predictive auto-regressive sort of token selection and even like with some temporal grounding, you\'ll still be prone to errors in like which team he plays for because not that long ago, only the last couple months he\'s moved to Barcelona. So there\'ll be much less information. There\'ll still be information about him at Manchester City in 2026 because he was playing for them in the last season. This season he plays for Barcelona, so be aware of that trap and use that sort of principle when checking players and teams they play for in Futbin only from EA Sports FC 27. No previous games, only 27, nothing before that, no 26, no 25, no 24. And only looking at squads and player overalls, player stats, everything from the 26-27 season. And ground every piece of information from the exact moment that I prompt or that you are answering, including the time. So right now it\'s 12:00, it\'s 9:12 on the 21st of October 2026. Therefore, ground your search, your information, everything for every search, for every answer in the exact time T equals zero. So it\'s a Wednesday today. So if I say like Arsenal are playing tonight, you know that means that Arsenal playing on Wednesday. You should be able to understand and know the day that they are playing and work out that that\'s today. Always use British Standard Time and yeah. Ground it, be able to work out the time that it currently is and therefore how long it is until the game.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]\nPlan action: revise\nPlan path: .aistudio/artifacts/2a7c5f08-d758-4a0e-bf87-d352fd259607/implementation_plan.md\nInstructions: If you need more context from the user you must first ask the user some questions using the ask_question tool. If you ask questions, you must wait for the user\'s answers. After the questions you must present a plan.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_END]"
